<TopicPage slug="subjects/physiotherapy/foundational/the-movement-sciences">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Physiotherapy is applied movement science. Behind every manipulation, exercise programme and
    gait re-education sit three intersecting disciplines: <strong>biomechanics</strong> (the body
    as a system of levers and loads), <strong>exercise physiology</strong> (the body as an energy
    system that adapts to demand) and <strong>motor control and learning</strong> (the nervous
    system as the orchestrator that must actually produce the movement). A clinician who can read
    a movement problem in all three languages can tell whether a patient's difficulty is weak,
    stiff, unskilled — or a mix — and prescribe accordingly. This lesson walks the terrain the
    first syllabus unit maps: kinesiology and biomechanics, exercise physiology, and how movement
    is learned.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the working equations of the treatment room: torque, work, power and training heart rate" widget="equationStep" config={torqueCfg} />

  <h2>Kinesiology and biomechanics</h2>
  <p>
    Kinesiology describes movement; biomechanics explains it with forces. The skeleton is a
    lever system — mostly <strong>third-class levers</strong>, where the muscle inserts between
    joint and load, sacrificing force for speed and range. Torque is therefore the currency of
    joint function, and it changes with angle. The figure below shows why: hold a weight with the
    forearm horizontal and the load's moment arm is longest, so external torque peaks near 90°;
    rotate toward vertical and the same load becomes almost effortless. Muscle has its own
    geometry-dependent behaviour — the <strong>force–length relationship</strong> (peak tension
    near mid-range, where actin–myosin overlap is optimal) and the <strong>force–velocity
    relationship</strong> (Hill's classic curve: force falls as shortening speed rises). Clinical
    testing angles, isokinetic dynamometry and manual muscle test positions all exist because
    strength is not a single number but a curve.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="external torque of a held load rises and falls with the cosine of joint angle — drag the sliders" widget="plotter" config={elbowCfg} />

  <h2>Exercise physiology</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Three energy systems in series.</strong> The phosphagen system (ATP–PCr) fuels about 10 seconds of maximal effort; anaerobic glycolysis bridges tens of seconds to a couple of minutes at the cost of lactate and hydrogen ions; oxidative metabolism carries everything beyond, limited by oxygen delivery and mitochondrial density.</li>
    <li><strong>VO<sub>2</sub>max is the ceiling.</strong> Maximal oxygen uptake (ml/kg/min) indexes cardiorespiratory capacity; training raises it 15–25% through central (stroke volume) and peripheral (capillary, mitochondrial) adaptations.</li>
    <li><strong>Dose makes the adaptation.</strong> Intensity, duration, frequency and progression follow the FITT-VP principle; the Karvonen target heart rate above is the classic bedside translation. Deconditioning obeys the same law in reverse — bed rest strips aerobic capacity within weeks, which is why early mobilisation is a prescription, not a courtesy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Motor control and learning</h2>
  <p>
    Strength and fitness are useless if the nervous system cannot deploy them. Fitts and
    Posner's stages — <strong>cognitive, associative, autonomous</strong> — describe every
    patient's journey from thinking through a movement to doing it while talking. Schmidt's
    schema theory explains why variable practice beats repetition: the motor program is tuned by
    comparing predicted with actual sensory consequences. Practice design is therefore a
    clinical decision — <strong>blocked practice</strong> builds fast session performance,
    <strong>random practice</strong> builds retention and transfer (contextual interference),
    and feedback shifts from every-trial extrinsic knowledge of results to the patient's own
    intrinsic error detection. The end goal of therapy is not a better Wednesday session; it is
    a movement the patient owns at home.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Norkin &amp; Levangie, Joint Structure and Function; McArdle, Katch &amp; Katch, Exercise Physiology; Shumway-Cook &amp; Woollacott, Motor Control: Translating Research into Clinical Practice.</p>
</TopicPage>
